/* Generated from data/labranges.json - do not edit by hand. */
window.LABRANGE_DATA = {"note":"Normal and optimal ranges written for this tool from standard clinical references. `normal` is the conventional adult reference range or the description of a normal study; `optimal` is the narrower functional target where one is defensible. Reference intervals are assay-, method-, age- and sex-specific: the range printed on the report in front of you governs.","ranges":[{"id":"cbc","units":null,"normal":"Haemoglobin 13.5–17.5 g/dL (men), 12.0–15.5 (women). Haematocrit 41–53% / 36–46%. WBC 4.5–11.0 ×10⁹/L. Platelets 150–400 ×10⁹/L. MCV 80–100 fL. Differential: neutrophils 40–60%, lymphocytes 20–40%, monocytes 2–8%, eosinophils 1–4%, basophils 0.5–1%.","optimal":"Haemoglobin mid-range rather than at either limit; MCV 82–89 fL; WBC 5.0–7.5 ×10⁹/L; neutrophil-to-lymphocyte ratio under 2.0; platelets 175–250 ×10⁹/L.","note":"A high-normal MCV with a low-normal B12 is the pattern that gets missed."},{"id":"cmp","units":null,"normal":"Sodium 135–145 mmol/L. Potassium 3.5–5.2. Chloride 98–107. CO₂ 22–29. BUN 7–20 mg/dL. Creatinine 0.7–1.3 mg/dL (men), 0.6–1.1 (women). eGFR >60 mL/min/1.73m². Fasting glucose 70–99 mg/dL. Calcium 8.6–10.2 mg/dL. Albumin 3.5–5.0 g/dL. Total protein 6.0–8.3 g/dL.","optimal":"Fasting glucose 75–86 mg/dL. BUN 10–16 mg/dL. eGFR >90. Albumin 4.2–5.0 g/dL (a marker of protein status and hepatic synthesis). CO₂ 25–30 mmol/L. Potassium 4.0–4.5 mmol/L.","note":"Correct calcium for albumin: add 0.8 mg/dL per 1.0 g/dL that albumin sits below 4.0."},{"id":"a1c","units":"%","normal":"Under 5.7% normal. 5.7–6.4% prediabetes. 6.5% or above is diagnostic of diabetes on two occasions.","optimal":"4.8–5.3%. Below 4.6% with normal glucose raises the question of shortened red cell survival rather than excellent control.","note":"Falsely low in haemolysis, recent blood loss, pregnancy and iron deficiency treatment; falsely high in iron deficiency anaemia and uraemia."},{"id":"lft","units":null,"normal":"ALT 7–56 U/L, AST 10–40 U/L, ALP 44–147 U/L, GGT 8–61 U/L (men) / 5–36 (women), total bilirubin 0.2–1.2 mg/dL, direct bilirubin under 0.3 mg/dL, albumin 3.5–5.0 g/dL.","optimal":"ALT under 30 U/L in men and under 19 U/L in women — the healthy-population upper limits, well below most laboratory ranges. AST under 25. GGT under 20 (it tracks oxidative stress and alcohol long before it tracks disease). Total bilirubin 0.4–1.0 mg/dL — mildly high unconjugated bilirubin is an antioxidant, not a problem.","note":"An isolated raised ALP needs fractionation or GGT to separate liver from bone."},{"id":"lipids","units":null,"normal":"Total cholesterol under 200 mg/dL. LDL-C under 100 mg/dL (under 70 in established cardiovascular disease). HDL-C above 40 mg/dL (men), above 50 (women). Triglycerides under 150 mg/dL. Non-HDL cholesterol under 130 mg/dL.","optimal":"Triglycerides under 100 mg/dL, and a triglyceride-to-HDL ratio under 2.0 (under 1.5 is better) — the single most useful insulin-resistance signal on a standard panel. HDL 55–80 mg/dL. ApoB under 80 mg/dL or LDL-P under 1,000 nmol/L where measured.","note":"A cholesterol under about 130 mg/dL is not a target: sterols are the substrate for steroid hormones and cell membranes."},{"id":"fastingins","units":null,"normal":"Fasting insulin 2–19.6 µIU/mL (laboratory range). Fasting glucose 70–99 mg/dL. HOMA-IR under 2.0.","optimal":"Fasting insulin 2–5 µIU/mL. Fasting glucose 75–86 mg/dL. HOMA-IR under 1.0. These move years before HbA1c or fasting glucose leave their reference ranges.","note":"HOMA-IR = fasting insulin (µIU/mL) × fasting glucose (mg/dL) ÷ 405."},{"id":"tsh","units":null,"normal":"TSH 0.45–4.5 mIU/L. Free T4 0.8–1.8 ng/dL.","optimal":"TSH 1.0–2.0 mIU/L. Free T4 in the upper half, 1.1–1.5 ng/dL. A TSH above 2.5 with positive antibodies is a different conversation from the same TSH without them.","note":"TSH is suppressed by biotin supplementation in some immunoassays — stop biotin 72 hours before drawing."},{"id":"t3rt3","units":null,"normal":"Free T3 2.3–4.2 pg/mL. Reverse T3 8–25 ng/dL.","optimal":"Free T3 in the upper third, 3.2–4.2 pg/mL. Reverse T3 under 15 ng/dL. Free T3 to reverse T3 ratio above 20 when both are expressed in the same units.","note":"Reverse T3 rises in acute illness, fasting, and severe stress — it reflects non-thyroidal illness far more often than a thyroid disorder."},{"id":"thyroidab","units":null,"normal":"Anti-TPO under 34 IU/mL. Anti-thyroglobulin under 40 IU/mL. Both cutoffs are assay-specific.","optimal":"Undetectable, or below the reporting limit. Any titre above the cutoff establishes autoimmunity even with a normal TSH.","note":"Up to 10% of healthy people carry low-titre TPO antibodies; the finding needs a clinical context."},{"id":"vitdlab","units":"ng/mL","normal":"Deficiency under 20 ng/mL (50 nmol/L). Insufficiency 20–29. Sufficiency 30–100.","optimal":"40–60 ng/mL (100–150 nmol/L). Above 100 ng/mL risks hypercalcaemia; above 150 is toxic.","note":"Check with calcium: a high-normal calcium with a high vitamin D warrants a parathyroid look."},{"id":"b12folate","units":null,"normal":"B12 200–900 pg/mL. Serum folate above 4 ng/mL. Methylmalonic acid under 0.40 µmol/L.","optimal":"B12 500–900 pg/mL. MMA under 0.27 µmol/L. Homocysteine 5–7 µmol/L. Functional B12 deficiency is common in the 200–400 pg/mL band — MMA and homocysteine settle it.","note":"Never correct folate without knowing B12 status: folate resolves the anaemia while the neuropathy progresses."},{"id":"ferritin","units":null,"normal":"Ferritin 24–336 ng/mL (men), 11–307 (women; many laboratories use 15–150). Serum iron 60–170 µg/dL. TIBC 240–450 µg/dL. Transferrin saturation 20–50%.","optimal":"Ferritin 50–150 ng/mL, and 50–100 in menstruating women. Transferrin saturation 25–35%.","note":"Ferritin is an acute phase reactant: a normal or high value with a raised CRP does not exclude iron deficiency. Saturation above 45% with a high ferritin points at haemochromatosis."},{"id":"crp","units":null,"normal":"hs-CRP under 3.0 mg/L. ESR under 15 mm/hr (men), under 20 (women); the rough age rule is age ÷ 2, and (age + 10) ÷ 2 in women.","optimal":"hs-CRP under 1.0 mg/L, and under 0.5 in a low-risk patient. ESR under 10 mm/hr.","note":"hs-CRP above 10 mg/L is an infection or an acute event, not a cardiovascular risk reading — repeat it when well."},{"id":"urate","units":"mg/dL","normal":"3.4–7.0 mg/dL (men), 2.4–6.0 (women).","optimal":"3.5–5.5 mg/dL. Treat-to-target in gout is under 6.0, and under 5.0 with tophi.","note":"Urate rises with fructose, alcohol, thiazides and low-dose aspirin; it can be normal during an acute attack."},{"id":"ck","units":"U/L","normal":"39–308 U/L (men), 26–192 (women).","optimal":"Under 200 U/L in an untrained person. Values rise for days after intense exercise — repeat after 72 hours of rest before acting.","note":"Statin myopathy: symptoms with CK above 5× the upper limit warrants stopping; rhabdomyolysis is typically above 10,000."},{"id":"trop","units":null,"normal":"High-sensitivity troponin T under 14 ng/L; high-sensitivity troponin I under 16 ng/L (women) and under 34 (men). Assay- and sex-specific.","optimal":null,"note":"A single normal value does not exclude infarction — serial testing at 0 and 3 hours, with the delta, is the algorithm. Chronic elevation occurs in renal failure and heart failure."},{"id":"bnp","units":null,"normal":"BNP under 100 pg/mL. NT-proBNP under 125 pg/mL under age 75, under 450 above it.","optimal":null,"note":"BNP under 35 pg/mL makes chronic heart failure very unlikely. Obesity lowers the values; renal impairment and atrial fibrillation raise them."},{"id":"ddimer","units":"ng/mL FEU","normal":"Under 500 ng/mL FEU.","optimal":null,"note":"Age-adjusted cutoff above 50 years: age × 10 ng/mL. A negative D-dimer only excludes thrombosis when pre-test probability is low or intermediate."},{"id":"procalcitonin","units":"ng/mL","normal":"Under 0.10 ng/mL: bacterial infection unlikely. 0.10–0.25: unlikely. 0.25–0.50: possible. Above 0.50: bacterial infection likely. Above 2.0: severe sepsis.","optimal":null,"note":"Used to stop antibiotics as much as to start them: a fall of 80% from peak supports discontinuation."},{"id":"eos","units":"cells/µL","normal":"Absolute eosinophils 0–500 cells/µL.","optimal":"Under 200 cells/µL in the absence of atopy.","note":"300 cells/µL or above predicts inhaled corticosteroid response in COPD. Above 1,500 is hypereosinophilia and needs a parasite, drug and malignancy workup."},{"id":"abg","units":null,"normal":"pH 7.35–7.45. PaCO₂ 35–45 mmHg. PaO₂ 80–100 mmHg. HCO₃⁻ 22–26 mEq/L. Base excess −2 to +2. SaO₂ 95–100%.","optimal":null,"note":"A-a gradient normal is (age ÷ 4) + 4. A normal or rising PaCO₂ in an acutely breathless asthmatic is an emergency, not reassurance."},{"id":"ana","units":null,"normal":"Negative, or a titre below 1:40. 1:80 is borderline. 1:160 or higher is clinically significant when read with the pattern.","optimal":null,"note":"Up to 15% of healthy people are ANA-positive at 1:40, more with age. The pattern — homogeneous, speckled, nucleolar, centromere — directs the extractable antigen panel."},{"id":"rf_ccp","units":null,"normal":"Rheumatoid factor under 14 IU/mL. Anti-CCP under 20 U/mL.","optimal":null,"note":"Anti-CCP is the specific one: RF appears in hepatitis C, Sjögren's, endocarditis and in healthy older people."},{"id":"celiac","units":null,"normal":"tTG-IgA below the assay cutoff (commonly under 4 U/mL). Total IgA 70–400 mg/dL.","optimal":null,"note":"The whole panel is invalid on a gluten-free diet. If total IgA is deficient — 2 to 3% of coeliacs — reflex to tTG-IgG and deamidated gliadin peptide IgG."},{"id":"hepser","units":null,"normal":"HBsAg negative. Anti-HBc negative. Anti-HBs above 10 mIU/mL indicates immunity. Anti-HCV negative; if reactive, confirm with HCV RNA.","optimal":null,"note":"Vaccinated: anti-HBs positive alone. Past infection: anti-HBc and anti-HBs positive."},{"id":"aat","units":"mg/dL","normal":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin 90–200 mg/dL. Below 57 mg/dL (11 µmol/L) is the protective threshold for emphysema.","optimal":null,"note":"It is an acute phase reactant: a normal level during inflammation can mask a deficiency. Phenotype or genotype settles it — MM normal, MZ carrier, ZZ severe deficiency."},{"id":"psa","units":"ng/mL","normal":"Under 4.0 ng/mL, or age-specific: under 2.5 at 40–49, under 3.5 at 50–59, under 4.5 at 60–69, under 6.5 at 70–79.","optimal":null,"note":"Velocity above 0.75 ng/mL per year and free PSA under 25% raise concern at any absolute value. 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors halve PSA — double the reading."},{"id":"androgens","units":null,"normal":"Total testosterone 300–1,000 ng/dL (men), 15–70 (women). Free testosterone 50–210 pg/mL (men). DHEA-S 80–560 µg/dL (men), 35–430 (women). SHBG 10–57 nmol/L (men), 18–144 (women).","optimal":"Total testosterone in the upper half for age in men. In women, a free androgen index under 5 — a higher index with a low SHBG is the PCOS pattern even when total testosterone is normal.","note":"Draw testosterone before 10 am, fasting, and repeat any abnormal result."},{"id":"hormones_f","units":null,"normal":"Estradiol 25–75 pg/mL follicular, 150–350 mid-cycle, 50–150 luteal, under 30 postmenopausal. Progesterone above 3 ng/mL confirms ovulation, above 10 is a robust luteal phase. FSH 3–10 IU/L follicular, above 25 postmenopausal. LH 2–10 IU/L follicular.","optimal":"Luteal progesterone 15–25 ng/mL. An LH-to-FSH ratio under 2 (above 2 with hyperandrogenism suggests PCOS). Estradiol not left unopposed by a short or absent luteal phase.","note":"Timing is everything: draw progesterone 7 days after ovulation, not on a fixed cycle day."},{"id":"prolactin","units":"ng/mL","normal":"4–23 ng/mL (women), 3–15 (men).","optimal":null,"note":"Stress, nipple stimulation, exercise and a recent meal all raise it — repeat before acting. Above 200 ng/mL suggests a macroprolactinoma; between 25 and 100, check medications and thyroid first."},{"id":"ua","units":null,"normal":"pH 4.5–8.0. Specific gravity 1.005–1.030. Protein, glucose, ketones, bilirubin, nitrites and leukocyte esterase all negative. RBC under 3/hpf. WBC under 5/hpf. No casts other than occasional hyaline.","optimal":"First-morning pH 6.0–6.5 (a persistently acidic urine favours urate stones). Specific gravity 1.010–1.020 as a hydration marker.","note":"Squamous cells above 5/hpf mean a contaminated specimen — recollect clean-catch before treating."},{"id":"utox","units":null,"normal":"Negative for the panel tested.","optimal":null,"note":"Immunoassay screens cross-react; confirm any positive with GC-MS or LC-MS before it goes in a record."},{"id":"legionella_ag","units":null,"normal":"Negative for both Legionella pneumophila serogroup 1 antigen and pneumococcal antigen.","optimal":null,"note":"The Legionella test only detects serogroup 1 — around 80% of cases. A negative does not exclude legionellosis."},{"id":"fob","units":null,"normal":"Negative. Quantitative FIT under 100 ng/mL haemoglobin (thresholds vary by kit).","optimal":null,"note":"FIT detects human globin and needs no dietary restriction; guaiac testing does. A positive result means colonoscopy, not a repeat test."},{"id":"calpro","units":"µg/g","normal":"Under 50 µg/g normal. 50–200 borderline, repeat or investigate. Above 200 suggests inflammatory bowel disease.","optimal":"Under 20 µg/g.","note":"NSAIDs, PPIs, infection and age all raise it. It is the single best test for separating IBS from IBD without a scope."},{"id":"stoolcx","units":null,"normal":"No enteric pathogens isolated; no ova, cysts or parasites seen; PCR panel negative.","optimal":null,"note":"Three specimens on separate days for ova and parasites — a single sample misses Giardia around 30% of the time. PCR panels detect carriage as well as disease."},{"id":"sputum","units":null,"normal":"A valid specimen has under 10 squamous epithelial cells and over 25 neutrophils per low-power field. Normal flora only on culture.","optimal":null,"note":"A specimen that fails the cell criteria is saliva, and its culture means nothing."},{"id":"afb","units":null,"normal":"No acid-fast bacilli on three sputum smears; TB nucleic acid amplification negative; culture negative at 6 to 8 weeks.","optimal":null,"note":"Smear sensitivity is around 50 to 60% — culture and NAAT decide. NAAT also reports rifampicin resistance."},{"id":"urineculture","units":null,"normal":"No growth, or under 10⁴ CFU/mL of a single organism. Significant bacteriuria is 10⁵ CFU/mL of a single uropathogen (10³ in a symptomatic woman on a clean-catch specimen).","optimal":null,"note":"Asymptomatic bacteriuria is treated only in pregnancy and before urological instrumentation."},{"id":"sti","units":null,"normal":"Negative nucleic acid amplification for Chlamydia trachomatis, Neisseria gonorrhoeae, Trichomonas and Mycoplasma genitalium as tested.","optimal":null,"note":"Test the exposed site — pharyngeal and rectal infections are missed entirely by a urine NAAT."},{"id":"wetmount","units":null,"normal":"Vaginal pH 3.8–4.5. Lactobacilli predominant. No clue cells, no trichomonads, no hyphae or budding yeast. Few white cells.","optimal":"pH 4.0–4.5 with a lactobacillus-dominant field.","note":"pH above 4.5 with clue cells is bacterial vaginosis; pH above 4.5 with motile flagellates is trichomonas; a normal pH with hyphae is candida."},{"id":"kohprep","units":null,"normal":"No fungal elements — no hyphae, pseudohyphae or spores. Fungal culture: no growth at 4 weeks.","optimal":null,"note":"Stop topical antifungals for a week before scraping, or the preparation is falsely negative."},{"id":"nasalswab","units":null,"normal":"Normal nasal flora; no Staphylococcus aureus, MRSA or pathogenic mould isolated.","optimal":null,"note":"Staph aureus carriage is present in around 30% of healthy adults — colonisation is not infection."},{"id":"spirometry","units":null,"normal":"FEV1/FVC ratio 0.70 or above, or above the lower limit of normal for age. FEV1 80% or more of predicted. FVC 80% or more of predicted. Significant bronchodilator response is a rise in FEV1 of 12% AND 200 mL.","optimal":"FEV1 and FVC at or above 100% of predicted, with an FEV1/FVC of 0.75 to 0.85 in a young adult.","note":"GOLD severity by FEV1 % predicted once the ratio is obstructive: mild 80+, moderate 50–79, severe 30–49, very severe under 30. A reduced ratio with a normal FVC is obstruction; both reduced suggests restriction and needs lung volumes."},{"id":"peakflow","units":"L/min","normal":"Predicted adult peak flow is roughly 500–700 L/min in men and 380–500 in women, by height and age. The personal best matters more than the predicted value.","optimal":"Consistently 80% or more of personal best, with under 20% diurnal variability.","note":"Zones: green 80–100% of personal best, yellow 50–79% (step up treatment), red under 50% (rescue treatment and urgent review)."},{"id":"feno","units":"ppb","normal":"Adults: under 25 ppb low, 25–50 intermediate, above 50 high. Children under 12: under 20, 20–35, above 35.","optimal":"Under 20 ppb in an adult on treatment.","note":"FeNO measures eosinophilic, corticosteroid-responsive airway inflammation. It falls with steroids and smoking, and rises with allergen exposure and nitrate-rich meals."},{"id":"pulseox","units":"%","normal":"SpO₂ 95–100% on room air at rest. Under 88% qualifies for supplemental oxygen. A fall of 4% or more on exertion is abnormal.","optimal":"97–99% at rest, with no exertional desaturation on a 6-minute walk.","note":"Pulse oximetry overestimates true saturation in darker skin pigmentation, and reads normally in carbon monoxide poisoning and methaemoglobinaemia."},{"id":"fet","units":"seconds","normal":"Under 6 seconds of forced expiration.","optimal":"3 to 5 seconds.","note":"Longer than 6 seconds correlates with airflow obstruction; longer than 9 seconds is strongly suggestive."},{"id":"abi","units":null,"normal":"1.00–1.40 normal. 0.91–0.99 borderline. 0.90 or below is diagnostic of peripheral arterial disease. Above 1.40 means non-compressible, calcified vessels.","optimal":"1.00–1.20.","note":"0.41–0.90 mild to moderate disease; 0.40 or below is severe, with rest pain and tissue loss."},{"id":"bp_home","units":"mmHg","normal":"Home average under 135/85 mmHg (equivalent to an office reading of 140/90). Daytime ambulatory average under 135/85, night-time under 120/70.","optimal":"115–120 / 70–80 mmHg, with a nocturnal dip of 10 to 20%.","note":"Average 7 days of duplicate morning and evening readings and discard day one. A non-dipping nocturnal pattern is an independent cardiovascular risk marker."},{"id":"orthostatic","units":null,"normal":"Less than a 20 mmHg fall in systolic or a 10 mmHg fall in diastolic pressure within 3 minutes of standing, with a heart rate rise under 20 bpm.","optimal":"A heart rate rise under 10 bpm and no symptomatic drop.","note":"A sustained heart rate rise of 30 bpm or more (40 or more in adolescents) without hypotension over 10 minutes defines POTS."},{"id":"sibo","units":null,"normal":"A rise in hydrogen of less than 20 ppm over baseline within 90 minutes, and methane under 10 ppm at every time point.","optimal":null,"note":"A rise of 20 ppm or more in hydrogen within 90 minutes is positive; methane of 10 ppm or more at any point indicates intestinal methanogen overgrowth. Use glucose or lactulose, not both, and prepare with 24 hours of a low-fermentation diet."},{"id":"cxr","units":null,"normal":"Cardiothoracic ratio under 0.5 on a PA film. Clear lung fields, sharp costophrenic angles, no infiltrate, effusion, pneumothorax or mass. Trachea midline, mediastinum not widened, no free subdiaphragmatic air.","optimal":null,"note":"A lateral film is needed to see retrocardiac and posterior costophrenic disease. Up to 300 mL of pleural fluid can hide on a PA view."},{"id":"chestct","units":null,"normal":"No pulmonary nodule above 6 mm. No ground-glass opacity, consolidation, bronchiectasis, honeycombing or mediastinal lymphadenopathy above 10 mm short axis.","optimal":null,"note":"Fleischner follow-up for an incidental solid nodule in a low-risk patient: under 6 mm no routine follow-up; 6–8 mm CT at 6–12 months; above 8 mm CT, PET-CT or biopsy at 3 months."},{"id":"ctpa","units":null,"normal":"No intraluminal filling defect in the pulmonary arteries. Right-ventricle-to-left-ventricle diameter ratio under 0.9.","optimal":null,"note":"An RV:LV ratio of 0.9 or above indicates right heart strain and changes the risk stratification of a confirmed embolism."},{"id":"echo","units":null,"normal":"Left ventricular ejection fraction 55–70%. LV wall thickness 6–11 mm. Left atrial diameter under 4.0 cm. E/e′ under 8. Tricuspid regurgitant velocity under 2.8 m/s (estimated pulmonary artery systolic pressure under 35 mmHg). Valves structurally normal with no more than trivial regurgitation.","optimal":"Ejection fraction 60–65% with normal diastolic indices and a normal left atrial volume index (under 34 mL/m²).","note":"An E/e′ above 14 with left atrial enlargement is the pattern of heart failure with preserved ejection fraction — a normal EF does not exclude heart failure."},{"id":"carotid_us","units":null,"normal":"Intima-media thickness under 0.9 mm. No plaque. Peak systolic velocity under 125 cm/s, and an internal carotid to common carotid ratio under 2.0 — under 50% stenosis.","optimal":"Intima-media thickness under 0.7 mm, or below the 25th percentile for age and sex.","note":"Peak systolic velocity 125–230 cm/s is 50–69% stenosis; above 230 cm/s is 70% or more, the surgical threshold in symptomatic disease."},{"id":"venous_us","units":null,"normal":"Full compressibility of the deep veins, phasic respiratory flow, competent valves with no reflux above 0.5 seconds in the superficial veins or 1.0 second in the deep veins. No echogenic thrombus.","optimal":null,"note":"A negative proximal ultrasound with ongoing suspicion is repeated at 5 to 7 days to catch propagating calf thrombus."},{"id":"aaa_us","units":null,"normal":"Infrarenal aortic diameter under 3.0 cm.","optimal":null,"note":"3.0–4.4 cm small aneurysm, rescan yearly; 4.5–5.4 cm rescan every 3 to 6 months; 5.5 cm or above, or growth above 1 cm per year, is a surgical referral."},{"id":"abdo_us","units":null,"normal":"Liver span under 15–16 cm with homogeneous echotexture and no steatosis. Gallbladder wall under 3 mm with no stones or sludge. Common bile duct under 6 mm (up to 8 mm after cholecystectomy or with age). Spleen under 12–13 cm. Portal vein under 13 mm. Pancreas normal in calibre.","optimal":null,"note":"Ultrasound detects steatosis only above roughly 20 to 30% fat infiltration; transient elastography or MRI-PDFF is more sensitive and grades fibrosis."},{"id":"renal_us","units":null,"normal":"Kidney length 9–13 cm with a cortical thickness above 1 cm and normal corticomedullary differentiation. No hydronephrosis, stone or mass. Post-void residual under 50 mL.","optimal":"Post-void residual under 30 mL, with less than 2 cm of length difference between the kidneys.","note":"A post-void residual above 100 mL is abnormal, and above 300 mL risks obstructive nephropathy. Small echogenic kidneys mean chronic disease and change the workup."},{"id":"pelvic_us","units":null,"normal":"Endometrial thickness under 4–5 mm postmenopausal; 4–8 mm proliferative and 7–16 mm secretory in the premenopausal cycle. Ovarian volume under 10 mL. Uterus 7–9 cm long with homogeneous myometrium.","optimal":null,"note":"Polycystic ovarian morphology is 20 or more follicles of 2–9 mm per ovary, or an ovarian volume of 10 mL or more — and morphology alone is not the diagnosis. Postmenopausal bleeding with an endometrium above 4 mm needs sampling."},{"id":"mammo","units":null,"normal":"BI-RADS 1 (negative) or 2 (benign finding). Breast density reported a to d, with c and d reducing mammographic sensitivity.","optimal":null,"note":"BI-RADS 3 is a 6-month short-interval follow-up; 4 and 5 mean biopsy. Dense breasts (c or d) may warrant supplemental ultrasound or MRI."},{"id":"dexa","units":null,"normal":"T-score of −1.0 or above is normal. −1.0 to −2.5 is osteopenia. −2.5 or below is osteoporosis. In premenopausal women and men under 50, use the Z-score: −2.0 or below is below the expected range for age.","optimal":"T-score above 0 at the lumbar spine and femoral neck.","note":"Scan the same machine each time — cross-machine comparisons are not valid. Degenerative change and aortic calcification falsely raise lumbar spine readings in older adults."},{"id":"mri_joint","units":null,"normal":"Intact articular cartilage of uniform thickness, no bone marrow oedema, no meniscal or labral tear, intact ligaments and tendons, no effusion beyond a physiological trace.","optimal":null,"note":"Structural findings are common in asymptomatic people — meniscal tears in around 30% over 50 — so the report never outranks the examination."},{"id":"mri_spine","units":null,"normal":"Preserved disc height and hydration, no disc herniation or annular tear, no central canal or foraminal stenosis, no facet arthropathy, normal cord signal, no marrow replacement.","optimal":null,"note":"Disc bulges are present in 30% of asymptomatic 20-year-olds and over 80% of 80-year-olds. Image for red flags, radiculopathy, or planned intervention — not for uncomplicated back pain."},{"id":"xray_joint","units":null,"normal":"Preserved joint space, no osteophytes, no subchondral sclerosis or cysts, normal alignment, no erosion, fracture or periosteal reaction.","optimal":null,"note":"Kellgren-Lawrence grades osteoarthritis 0 to 4; radiographic severity correlates poorly with pain. Erosions at the joint margin distinguish inflammatory from degenerative arthritis."},{"id":"ctabdo","units":null,"normal":"No free air or free fluid. Bowel wall under 3 mm and normally distended. Appendix under 6 mm. No abscess, mass or lymphadenopathy above 10 mm short axis. Solid organs normal.","optimal":null,"note":"A CT abdomen and pelvis delivers roughly 10 mSv — about 3 years of background radiation. Justify it, particularly in young patients and in repeated imaging."},{"id":"ekg","units":null,"normal":"Rate 60–100 bpm. PR interval 120–200 ms. QRS duration under 120 ms. QTc under 440 ms (men), under 460 (women). Axis −30° to +90°. Sinus rhythm with normal P wave morphology, no ST elevation or depression, no pathological Q waves.","optimal":"Resting rate 55–70 bpm with normal intervals and no conduction delay.","note":"A QTc above 500 ms is a torsades risk — review every QT-prolonging drug on the list. A normal EKG does not exclude ischaemia."},{"id":"holter","units":null,"normal":"Sinus rhythm with normal rate variation. Premature ventricular complex burden under 1% of total beats. No pauses above 3 seconds, no non-sustained ventricular tachycardia, no atrial fibrillation.","optimal":"PVC burden under 0.1%, with a normal heart rate variability profile.","note":"A PVC burden above 10% carries a risk of tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy and needs an echocardiogram."},{"id":"psg","units":null,"normal":"Apnoea-hypopnoea index under 5 events per hour. Oxygen desaturation index under 5. SpO₂ nadir above 90%. Sleep efficiency above 85%, with normal sleep architecture and no periodic limb movements.","optimal":"AHI under 2 with an SpO₂ nadir above 94% and 20–25% of the night in slow-wave sleep.","note":"AHI 5–15 mild, 15–30 moderate, above 30 severe. Central apnoeas are scored separately and point at heart failure, opioids or altitude rather than at the airway."},{"id":"rhc","units":null,"normal":"Mean pulmonary artery pressure 20 mmHg or below. Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure 15 mmHg or below. Pulmonary vascular resistance under 2 Wood units. Right atrial pressure under 8 mmHg. Cardiac index 2.5–4.0 L/min/m².","optimal":null,"note":"Pulmonary hypertension is a mean PAP above 20 mmHg. Pre-capillary disease has a wedge of 15 or below with PVR above 2; a higher wedge means the left heart is the cause."},{"id":"egd","units":null,"normal":"Normal oesophageal mucosa with a sharp Z-line at the gastro-oesophageal junction, normal gastric folds and antrum, normal duodenal villous pattern. No ulceration, varices, erosion or Barrett's segment.","optimal":null,"note":"Take four-quadrant biopsies for coeliac disease and for Barrett's surveillance even when the mucosa looks normal — patchy disease is missed on inspection alone."},{"id":"colonoscopy","units":null,"normal":"Normal mucosa to the caecum with an identified ileocaecal valve and appendiceal orifice. No polyps, masses, ulceration, inflammation or diverticular complication. Adequate bowel preparation (Boston score 6 or above, with each segment 2 or above).","optimal":null,"note":"Quality metrics: caecal intubation rate above 95%, adenoma detection rate above 30% in men and 20% in women, withdrawal time 6 minutes or more. A poor preparation means an early repeat, not a normal result."},{"id":"jointasp","units":null,"normal":"Clear, straw-coloured, viscous fluid. WBC under 200/µL with under 25% neutrophils. No crystals. Gram stain and culture negative.","optimal":null,"note":"200–2,000/µL non-inflammatory; 2,000–50,000 inflammatory; above 50,000 with over 90% neutrophils is septic until proven otherwise. Negatively birefringent needles are urate; positively birefringent rhomboids are calcium pyrophosphate."},{"id":"thoracentesis","units":null,"normal":"A transudate by Light's criteria: pleural-to-serum protein ratio under 0.5, pleural-to-serum LDH ratio under 0.6, and pleural LDH under two-thirds of the serum upper limit. Glucose above 60 mg/dL, pH above 7.30.","optimal":null,"note":"Any one of Light's criteria being met makes it an exudate. A pH under 7.20 with a low glucose in a parapneumonic effusion is an empyema and needs a chest drain."},{"id":"skinbiopsy","units":null,"normal":"Normal epidermal maturation and thickness, intact basement membrane, no interface change, no inflammatory infiltrate, normal adnexal structures.","optimal":null,"note":"Choose the technique for the question: shave for epidermal lesions, punch for inflammatory dermatoses (take the edge of an active lesion), excisional for pigmented lesions."},{"id":"cortisol","units":null,"normal":"Salivary cortisol on waking 3.7–9.5 ng/mL, falling through the day to under 1.0 ng/mL at midnight. Dried urine free cortisol within the laboratory's own diurnal reference bands.","optimal":"A cortisol awakening response of a 50% or greater rise 30 minutes after waking, a smooth diurnal decline, and a midnight value at the floor of the range.","note":"Four to five timed samples, not one. Shift work, corticosteroid inhalers and topical steroids all distort it. This does not diagnose Addison's or Cushing's — those need serum cortisol, ACTH and dynamic testing."},{"id":"oat","units":null,"normal":"All markers within the laboratory's reference intervals: organic acids of energy production, neurotransmitter turnover, detoxification, and microbial metabolites.","optimal":null,"note":"Interpretation is pattern-based and the evidence base is thin — elevated markers are hypotheses to test against symptoms and standard laboratory work, not diagnoses."},{"id":"gimap","units":null,"normal":"Commensal bacteria abundant and diverse; no bacterial, parasitic or viral pathogens. Pancreatic elastase above 200 µg/g. Secretory IgA within reference. Calprotectin under 50 µg/g. No occult blood.","optimal":"Pancreatic elastase above 500 µg/g. Calprotectin under 20 µg/g. Secretory IgA in the middle of the range — both extremes matter.","note":"Pancreatic elastase under 200 µg/g indicates exocrine insufficiency, and under 100 is severe. PCR panels report carriage as readily as disease."},{"id":"hpylori_t","units":null,"normal":"Urea breath test negative; stool antigen negative; histology negative on biopsy.","optimal":null,"note":"Stop PPIs for 2 weeks and antibiotics or bismuth for 4 weeks before testing, or the result is falsely negative. Serology cannot distinguish current from past infection — do not use it to confirm eradication."},{"id":"candida_ab","units":null,"normal":"Candida IgG, IgA and IgM all below the assay cutoff.","optimal":null,"note":"Antibodies indicate exposure, which is near-universal — they do not establish overgrowth. Read them only alongside culture or PCR on stool and the clinical picture."},{"id":"ige","units":null,"normal":"Total IgE under 100 IU/mL in adults. Specific IgE class 0, under 0.35 kU/L. Skin prick: a wheal under 3 mm larger than the negative control.","optimal":null,"note":"Sensitisation is not allergy: a positive test without a clinical reaction is not a reason to remove a food. Total IgE rises in parasitic infection and atopic dermatitis."},{"id":"hladq","units":null,"normal":"HLA-DQ2 (DQA1*05 / DQB1*02) and HLA-DQ8 (DQB1*03:02) both absent.","optimal":null,"note":"This test excludes rather than confirms: a negative result makes coeliac disease essentially impossible, but 30 to 40% of the general population carries DQ2 or DQ8 and never develops it."},{"id":"sweatcl","units":"mmol/L","normal":"Under 30 mmol/L: cystic fibrosis unlikely at any age. 30–59: intermediate, requires genetic testing. 60 or above: diagnostic.","optimal":null,"note":"Needs an adequate sweat sample (75 mg by Macroduct or 15 µL/min) from an accredited centre; a quantity-insufficient result is not a negative."},{"id":"semen","units":null,"normal":"WHO 6th edition lower reference limits: volume 1.4 mL or more, concentration 16 million/mL or more, total count 39 million or more, total motility 42% or more, progressive motility 30% or more, normal morphology 4% or more, vitality 54% or more.","optimal":"Concentration above 40 million/mL with progressive motility above 50% and normal morphology above 10%.","note":"These are lower reference limits from fertile men, not thresholds for fertility. Two samples 2 to 7 weeks apart, each after 2 to 7 days of abstinence, before any conclusion."},{"id":"heavymetals","units":null,"normal":"Blood lead under 3.5 µg/dL (the CDC reference value for children). Blood mercury under 5 µg/L. Urine arsenic under 35 µg/L total, with speciation to separate inorganic from harmless seafood arsenic. Urine cadmium under 1 µg/g creatinine.","optimal":"Blood lead under 1 µg/dL and mercury under 2 µg/L — there is no threshold below which lead is known to be safe.","note":"Provoked or challenge urine testing after a chelating agent has no validated reference range and is not recommended — an elevated post-challenge result is expected in anyone."},{"id":"mycotoxin","units":null,"normal":"Urinary mycotoxins below the laboratory's own cutoffs.","optimal":null,"note":"Cutoffs are laboratory-specific and not standardised between them; dietary mycotoxin exposure and renal function both affect excretion. Treat a raised result as a prompt to investigate the building, not as a diagnosis."},{"id":"shoemaker","units":null,"normal":"MSH 35–81 pg/mL. VIP 23–63 pg/mL. TGF-β1 under 2,380 pg/mL. C4a under 2,830 ng/mL. MMP-9 85–332 ng/mL. VEGF 31–86 pg/mL. Leptin 0.5–13.8 ng/mL (men), 3.7–11.1 (women). ADH 1.0–13.3 pg/mL with serum osmolality 280–300 mOsm/kg.","optimal":null,"note":"These cutoffs come from Dr Ritchie Shoemaker's own protocol and are not in general clinical use; several of the assays are research-grade and behave differently between laboratories."},{"id":"envtest","units":null,"normal":"ERMI score under 2 is low. HERTSMI-2 under 11 is considered safe for a susceptible occupant. Indoor airborne spore counts lower than, and of similar species to, the outdoor control sample.","optimal":null,"note":"A visible or smelled water intrusion outranks any number. ERMI was developed as a research tool and the manufacturer does not validate it for clinical decisions."},{"id":"phq9","units":"score 0–27","normal":"0–4 none or minimal. 5–9 mild. 10–14 moderate. 15–19 moderately severe. 20–27 severe.","optimal":"Under 5, with a negative item 9.","note":"A drop of 5 points, or of 50%, is the standard definition of treatment response. Item 9 outranks the total at any score."},{"id":"gad7","units":"score 0–21","normal":"0–4 minimal. 5–9 mild. 10–14 moderate. 15–21 severe.","optimal":"Under 5.","note":"A score of 10 or more is the recommended cutoff for further evaluation, with a sensitivity and specificity of about 89% and 82% for generalised anxiety disorder."},{"id":"mdq","units":"score 0–13","normal":"A positive screen requires 7 or more of the 13 symptom items, several occurring in the same period, and moderate or serious functional problems.","optimal":"Negative on all three criteria.","note":"Sensitivity around 70% in specialty settings and lower in primary care — a negative screen does not exclude bipolar disorder in a patient with recurrent depression."},{"id":"mmse","units":null,"normal":"MMSE 24–30 of 30 is normal (adjust for education). MoCA 26–30 of 30 is normal, with 1 point added for 12 or fewer years of education.","optimal":"MMSE 29–30; MoCA 28–30.","note":"MMSE 20–23 mild, 10–19 moderate, under 10 severe impairment. MoCA is the more sensitive of the two for mild cognitive impairment and detects what an MMSE of 27 misses."},{"id":"auditc","units":"score 0–12","normal":"0–2 negative. 3 or more is positive in women; 4 or more in men. 8 or more indicates hazardous or harmful use.","optimal":"0–2, or abstinence.","note":"The threshold is deliberately lower in women — the same intake produces higher blood alcohol at lower body water volume."},{"id":"epworth","units":"score 0–24","normal":"0–10 normal daytime sleepiness. 11–14 mild excessive sleepiness. 15–17 moderate. 18–24 severe.","optimal":"0–7.","note":"It measures sleepiness, not apnoea, and correlates poorly with the AHI — it does not gate a sleep study."},{"id":"stopbang","units":"score 0–8","normal":"0–2 low risk of obstructive sleep apnoea. 3–4 intermediate. 5–8 high risk.","optimal":"0–2.","note":"A score of 3 or more has around 93% sensitivity for moderate-to-severe apnoea, at the cost of specificity. A score of 5 or more warrants prioritised polysomnography."},{"id":"cat_score","units":"score 0–40","normal":"Under 10 low impact. 10–20 medium. 21–30 high. Above 30 very high impact.","optimal":"Under 10.","note":"A change of 2 points is the minimum clinically important difference. The CAT sits on the GOLD ABE axis with exacerbation history, not on its own."},{"id":"crista_q","units":null,"normal":"No structured cutoff — the instrument documents exposure history, symptom clusters and building history rather than producing a validated score.","optimal":null,"note":"Treat it as a structured intake, not as a diagnostic instrument. The objective work is the environmental testing and the standard laboratory panel."}]};
